--TEST--
A `Core`-owned class reached through an operand whose static type names no class renders through the very `toString` the written-out spelling calls, and one the spec gives no `toString` throws catchably instead — ADR 0028 § 1, ADR 0036 § 4
--FILE--
<?mwl
// ADR 0036 § 4: an operand typed `mixed` defers *which* class is behind the
// handle, so nothing is resolved where it is written and the rendering is
// decided by the concrete instance. For a declared class that is a virtual
// call into a compiled method table; a `Core` class has no entry in one, so
// its renderer is carried on the descriptor instead and
// `mwl_runtime::stringify` asks for that before the table.
//
// What this pins is the **agreement**, not the four answers: a spelling that
// grew a rendering of its own — or an erased path that recomposed a `Uri` out
// of its components rather than calling the member — still looks right on its
// own line and fails here. The sibling case
// `a-core-class-that-has-a-tostring-renders-through-it` asks the same question
// of the *static* spelling, and `$direct` below is the value both share.
class Erased {
    // Every rendering spelling there is, over one `mixed`, counted against the
    // member call written out on the operand's own declared binding.
    public static function agrees(mixed $m, string $direct): int {
        int $agree = 0;
        if ("$m" == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        if (("" . $m) == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        if (($m as string) == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        string $echoed = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo $m; }) as string;
        if ($echoed == $direct) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
        return $agree;
    }
}

Core\Uri $u = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/a?b=1");
Core\Uuid $id = Core\Uuid::parse("0191b0c4-1c2f-7a3d-8f4e-5a6b7c8d9e0f");
Core\Time\Duration $d = Core\Time\Duration::seconds(90);

mixed $mu = $u;
mixed $mid = $id;
mixed $md = $d;

echo Erased::agrees($mu, $u->toString()), " ",
     Erased::agrees($mid, $id->toString()), " ",
     Erased::agrees($md, $d->toString()), "\n";

echo $mu, "\n";
echo $mid, "\n";
echo $md, "\n";

// A native member borrows argument 0, so an operand with no other owner is the
// rendering site's to release — the same edge the static case pins, now
// reached through the erased path, where the reference belongs to a `mixed`
// binding rather than to a declared one.
mixed $fresh = Core\Uri::parse("https://fresh.example/p");
echo "fresh=" . $fresh, "\n";

// The other side of the bound. `mwl_stdlib::registry::class_renders` is what
// the checker asks at a written `echo $pattern`, and a `Core` class it answers
// `false` for is `E0710` there. Behind a `mixed` there is nothing to ask at
// compile time, so ADR 0036 § 4's rule applies instead: a checked, catchable
// throw, never a fatal and never a silent empty string.
mixed $pattern = Core\Regex::compile("a+");
try {
    echo $pattern, "\n";
    echo "rendered\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "refused\n";
}
--EXPECT--
4 4 4
https://example.com/a?b=1
0191b0c4-1c2f-7a3d-8f4e-5a6b7c8d9e0f
1m30s
fresh=https://fresh.example/p
refused
